# Rust Development Guidelines

## Error Handling

- **NO exception swallowing** - all errors must propagate with context
- Use `thiserror` for error types (see `popmix-core/src/error.rs`)
- Use the `Result<T>` type alias from `popmix_core::error`
- Add context to errors using descriptive struct variants

```rust
// Good: Explicit error with context
#[error("Population not found: '{0}'")]
PopulationNotFound(String),

// Good: Propagate with context
fn get_pop(&self, name: &str) -> Result<&Population> {
    self.populations.get(name)
        .ok_or_else(|| PopmixError::PopulationNotFound(name.to_string()))
}
```

## Parallelization

- Use `rayon` for parallel iteration
- Default thread count: 4 (configurable via `init_thread_pool`)
- Use `par_iter()` for embarrassingly parallel operations
- Be mindful of memory allocation in parallel loops

```rust
use rayon::prelude::*;

// Parallel computation over blocks
let results: Vec<_> = blocks.par_iter()
    .map(|block| compute_statistic(block))
    .collect();
```

## Code Style

- Run `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy` before committing
- Use `//!` doc comments for module-level documentation
- Use `///` doc comments for public items
- Group imports: std, external crates, internal modules

## Module Organization

```rust
// In lib.rs - re-export public API
pub mod error;
pub mod fstats;

// Re-exports for convenience
pub use error::{PopmixError, Result};
pub use fstats::{F2Blocks, F2Result};
```

## Testing

```bash
cargo test --workspace                    # All tests
cargo test --workspace -- --nocapture     # With output
cargo test -p popmix-core fst             # Specific tests
```

## Numerical Precision

Use `approx` crate for floating-point comparisons:

```rust
use approx::assert_relative_eq;

assert_relative_eq!(result, expected, epsilon = 1e-10);
```
